# KAF81 Website - Cursor Rules

## Project Overview
This is a static website for KAF81 (KASI 1981 Alumni Foundation) built with vanilla HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. No frameworks or libraries are used except for Google Fonts.

## Technology Stack
- HTML5 (Semantic)
- CSS3 (Vanilla, no preprocessors)
- JavaScript (Vanilla ES6+, no frameworks)
- Supabase (for contact form backend only)

## Code Style and Structure

### HTML
- Use semantic HTML5 elements (`<header>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<section>`, `<article>`, `<aside>`, `<footer>`)
- Proper heading hierarchy (h1 → h2 → h3, no skipping levels)
- Always include `lang="en"` on `<html>` tag
- Include viewport meta tag: `<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">`
- All images must have descriptive `alt` attributes
- Use descriptive link text (avoid "click here")
- Include skip to main content link for accessibility

### CSS
- Mobile-first responsive design approach
- Use CSS custom properties (CSS variables) for theming
- Typography:
  - Headings: `font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif;`
  - Body: `font-family: 'Quicksand', sans-serif;`
- Use CSS Grid and Flexbox for layouts
- Use meaningful class names (BEM-like but not strict BEM)
- Include focus states for all interactive elements
- Support `prefers-reduced-motion` media query
- Ensure color contrast ratio ≥ 4.5:1 for accessibility

### JavaScript
- Use vanilla JavaScript only (no libraries or frameworks)
- Use `'use strict';` in all JS files
- Use the `function` keyword for pure functions
- Use descriptive variable names with auxiliary verbs (e.g., `isLoading`, `hasError`)
- Prefer const over let, avoid var
- Use curly braces for all conditionals
- Write modular, reusable functions
- Use IIFE pattern where appropriate for scoping

## Code Organization

### File Structure
```
kaf81.org/
├── index.html
├── [other].html
├── css/
│   └── style.css
├── js/
│   ├── main.js
│   ├── contact.js
│   └── form-validation.js
└── images/
```

### Naming Conventions
- HTML files: lowercase with dashes (e.g., `apply-online.html`)
- CSS classes: lowercase with dashes (e.g., `.form-group`)
- JavaScript files: lowercase with dashes (e.g., `form-validation.js`)
- Images: lowercase with dashes (e.g., `scholarship-certificate.jpeg`)

## Response Constraints
- Do not remove any existing code unless necessary
- Do not remove comments or commented-out code unless necessary
- Do not change the formatting of existing code unless important for new functionality
- Do not change the formatting of imports unless necessary

## Accessibility Requirements (WCAG 2.1 AA)
- Use semantic HTML elements
- Include ARIA labels and roles where needed
- Ensure keyboard navigation works for all interactive elements
- Provide visible focus indicators
- Include skip navigation link
- Use proper form labels and error messages
- Maintain proper heading hierarchy
- Ensure color contrast meets AA standards
- Add `aria-current="page"` to current navigation items
- Use `aria-label` for icon-only buttons
- Include `role` attributes where semantic HTML isn't sufficient

## SEO Requirements
- Include unique meta descriptions on every page
- Use Open Graph tags for social sharing
- Use Twitter Card tags
- Include canonical URLs
- Add Schema.org structured data (JSON-LD):
  - Organization schema on homepage
  - FAQPage schema on FAQ page
  - BreadcrumbList schema on all pages
  - ContactPage schema on contact page
- All images must have descriptive alt text
- Use descriptive, keyword-rich page titles
- Include XML sitemap and robots.txt

## Performance Optimization (Lighthouse 100)
- Implement lazy loading for images (`loading="lazy"`)
- Optimize images (appropriate formats, sizes)
- Load Google Fonts with `display=swap` and preconnect
- Minimize and optimize CSS/JS (production)
- Use efficient CSS selectors
- Avoid render-blocking resources
- Defer non-critical JavaScript
- Use efficient data structures and algorithms
- Optimize network requests

## Form Handling
- Client-side validation before submission
- Accessible error messages (ARIA live regions)
- Loading states during submission
- Supabase integration for contact form
- Use vanilla fetch() API (no axios or other libraries)

## Best Practices
- Write concise, technical code
- Prefer functional and declarative programming patterns
- Avoid code duplication - use iteration and modularization
- Use descriptive variable names
- Structure files logically: HTML structure, then styling, then behavior
- Favor simplicity over cleverness
- Write code that is easy to understand and maintain

## Browser Support
- Support modern browsers (last 2 versions of Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge)
- Use feature detection where needed
- Provide fallbacks for unsupported features

## Testing Considerations
- Test keyboard navigation
- Test with screen readers
- Test on mobile devices
- Verify Lighthouse scores (aim for 100 in all categories)
- Test form validation and submission
- Verify all links work correctly
- Check color contrast ratios

## Documentation
- Update README.md when adding new features
- Comment complex code logic
- Document any external dependencies or setup requirements

## Git Practices
- Write clear, descriptive commit messages
- Don't commit temporary or build files
- Keep commits focused and atomic

## Specific Rules for This Project
- Do not add any CSS frameworks (Bootstrap, Tailwind, etc.)
- Do not add any JavaScript frameworks or libraries (React, Vue, jQuery, etc.)
- Do not use CSS preprocessors (SASS, LESS, etc.)
- Keep all code in the repository - no build steps required
- Use only HTML, CSS, and vanilla JavaScript
- Google Fonts are allowed (external resource)
- Supabase is allowed (backend service for contact form only)

